  "preamble": "The diphthongization of a long close-mid back rounded vowel /oː/ to a diphthong with a front offglide, /ɔɪ/, is documented in Yiddish, where Middle High German long ô became Standard (YIVO) and Polish Yiddish /ɔɪ/ (spelled ⟨וי⟩, romanized oy), while German kept /oː/ (MHG hôch > Yiddish hoykh, German hoch). Lithuanian Yiddish has [eɪ] instead, and Netherlandic Yiddish [ɔu].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Diphthongization, Vowel Breaking",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Yiddish",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Middle High German hōch 'high'",
          "to": "Yiddish הויך /hɔɪχ/",
          "note": "MHG long ō > Yiddish /ɔɪ/ (YIVO, Polish Yiddish); Lithuanian Yiddish [heɪχ]."
        },
        {
          "from": "Middle High German brōt 'bread'",
          "to": "Yiddish ברויט /brɔɪt/",
          "note": "Same development; Netherlandic Yiddish has [brɔut]."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old High German scōno 'beautifully'",
          "to": "Yiddish שוין /ʃɔɪn/ 'already'",
          "note": "The same long ō gives /ɔɪ/; compare German schon."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
